more than six million american women of  childbearing age have trouble getting or  staying pregnant author michelle t  struggled for three years to get  pregnant and went through multiple  rounds of ivf  instead of focusing on the stress and  anxiety t wanted to share the joy and  humor of her experience as a queer woman  in her 40s she gave birth to her son in  2014 and she writes all about it in her  new book knocking myself up a memoir of  my infertility michelle t good  morning um so your son is seven mm-hmm  why did you decide to write this book  now  well it really felt like now that he's a  fully formed little person who's in  school  um  i had enough time and space from those  early years which it was like one thing  after another you know the whole  struggle to get pregnant through ivf  that whole journey took up a lot and  then suddenly you have a little newborn  and that's your life and so there was a  moment where i could take a breath and  look back on that time with a little bit  of distance and felt like yeah i want to  dig into that so what was the hardest  part of your journey  i think that the hardest part was just  all of the unknowns you know like at  first i didn't even know if my body  could get pregnant  and then i learned that it couldn't and  i was really lucky because my partner at  the time also had ovaries and so i ended  up stealing one of their eggs and you  know going through ivf and doing that  those treatments and that's a lot of  unknowns too you know you're really in  the loop what did you learn about  yourself throughout this journey  i learned a tremendous amount about my  body that i didn't even know i mean i  started trying to get pregnant when i  was 40. so i figured i knew how this  whole thing worked but you know even  something like you know every month when  you you know when you drop an egg i  thought that's what happened you dropped  one little egg and that's you know how  you know you do or don't get pregnant  but in fact the ovaries produce all of  these eggs and then one rises to the top  and slays all the other  right yeah the powerful one so that was  really interesting and it made it really  explained my pms i was like there is a  battle to the death happening in here  so it makes sense yeah what was this  like navigating this process as a queer  woman because that's why i think this  book is so important for people uh to be  inspired by your journey  it was really challenging because  and this was shocking to me because as a  queer person i understood that a lot of  queer people have to seek out you know  artificial reproductive technologies if  they want to start a family so i  expected it to be a lot more  accommodating to queer people and while  the individuals that i worked with at  the clinic were wonderful and super  supportive it was like the system you  know the paperwork the computer programs  we're not so give us an example well one  example was because i was using my  partner at the times egg they had me  listed as a surrogate so all my  paperwork said like surrogate which was  you know no shade on surrogates they're  amazing and doing amazing work but  that's not what you were yeah it felt  really alienating you know to feel like  i was somehow outside of my family and  doing someone a favor so  there were things like that um  and so i was i was really shocked that  they weren't more accommodating your son  is seven now  does he know about sort of his backstory  does he have questions  um  i started telling my son the story of  his birth when he was very young um my  sperm donor is a friend okay and we call  him dhoni like there's your son right  there oh don't eat like auntie  yeah so we you know we say you know  dhoni so-and-so is coming over so that  he always knows that it's never a shock  that this other person was so  instrumental in his existence  and yeah he's always known the story and  only recently i had to explain  sometimes people do get pregnant outside  of a hospital  you know he's like oh i know how babies  happen you go to the you know to the  clinic  yeah there are other ways we'll get into  that someday but yeah what's your  message for people who are struggling to  get pregnant because it is remarkable  how often  i mean we see these stories increasingly  in the last 30 years that it's become  difficult and so what is your message to  those folks who are struggling to get  pregnant i mean it's all you can do is  persevere and be hopeful and try to stay  optimistic you know and and trust sort  of the technology i mean i was really  comforted by the fact that my doctor was  like we're going to get you pregnant  that's not you know we will get you  pregnant feel good that confidence yeah  i mean i was also like but at what  expense you know so it was also a little  like oh you know so uh but i do believe  that the technology works and i've heard  so many success stories from so many  people as a result of doing this kind of  writing trust the process huh yeah  michelle t thank you so much guys  knocking myself up is on sale now
 

